The results decompose the metric disagreement into two components. (A)~The crossing-rate shallow-layer preference is label-independent: 100\% of random-label repeats still select layers L0--L7, confirming that this bias reflects representation geometry (shallow-layer cosine distance compression; \cref{app:cosine_volatility}) rather than any property of the safety labels. The CR-composite inherits this bias (80\% shallow). (B)~Threshold-based metrics (precision, inverse FPR) select deep layers only with real safety labels; under random labels, selections scatter across the full layer range (mean L34--L36, high variance). This confirms that deep-layer probe performance reflects genuine safety-relevant structure. The full metric disagreement (L4 vs.\ L63, a 59-layer gap) is the superposition of a geometric artifact on the crossing-rate side and a real safety signal on the threshold-based side.


\section{Representation Compression Analysis}
\label{app:cosine_volatility}

We measure step-to-step cosine distance at CR-composite (shallow) vs.\ threshold-based (deep) layers across all models to characterize the compression mechanism underlying the crossing rate bias (\cref{tab:cosine_volatility}).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{Step-to-step cosine distance at CR-composite (shallow) and threshold-based (deep) layers. Shallow layers produce highly compressed representations (1.4--2.7$\times$ smaller inter-step distances).}
\label{tab:cosine_volatility}

\section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Layer selection as researcher degree of freedom~\citep{gelman2013garden}.}
Our five-metric ablation (\cref{sec:layer-results}) shows crossing rate reverses the central finding via shallow-layer compression (\cref{app:cosine_volatility}), architecture-independent across Llama/Qwen (\cref{tab:r18b_full_layer}). A random-label control on QwQ-32B reveals crossing rate retains its shallow preference in 100\% of random-label permutations (CR-composite: 80\%), while precision's deep preference collapses to 20\% (\cref{app:random_cp}): the crossing-rate shallow preference is geometric (persisting under random labels), while threshold-based deep selection requires genuine safety labels. Contribution~1 (layer bias) thus generalizes from geometry; Contribution~2 (HS advantage) is sample-dependent. Threshold-based layers peak at L63/64~\citep{wollschlager2025geometry}; R1-32B selectivity is 3.0pp (\cref{app:control}), below the 5pp threshold~\citep{hewitt2019control}.\looseness=-1

\paragraph{Encoder-quality boundary.}
The HS advantage is significant against 384d MiniLM (cross-validated at 7--8B; exploratory at 32B) but attenuated against 1024d BGE-large (1/4 HB). PCA capacity matching preserves significance (+16.8 to +23.1pp), partial evidence against a pure capacity explanation. Whether stronger encoders~\citep{wang2024e5mistral,boxo2025linear_probes} close the gap bounds these claims' shelf life.\looseness=-1

\paragraph{Guidance for future research.}
Converging evidence (oracle analysis, PCA capacity control, control task selectivity, position residualization) supports genuine safety representations, but causal validation via DAS~\citep{geiger2025causal}, ITI~\citep{li2024iti}, or circuit breakers~\citep{zou2024improving} is needed. Under linear residualization, 3/4 models retain significant non-positional HS advantage; under quadratic control, only 2/4 (QwQ-32B absorbed by $(t/T)^2$; \cref{tab:position_quadratic}). Quadratic control risks overfitting at $n{=}17$--$20$, so the divergent results bracket the true position contribution; HS probes partly functioning as better position encoders remains unexcluded. We propose a multi-metric audit protocol: compute ${\geq}3$ metrics, flag disagreement, and verify cosine volatility (\cref{app:cosine_volatility,app:deployment_operating_points}).\looseness=-1

\paragraph{Layer ensemble proof of concept.}
Training probes at the best layer per threshold-based metric (precision, BA, FPR) and averaging predictions yields a robust, metric-agnostic strategy: HS vs.\ text precision is significant for all four models ($p \leq 0.002$; \cref{tab:ensemble}), with +10.5 to +27.0pp advantages. The ensemble does not improve over oracle single-metric selection ($p > 0.27$) but eliminates metric pre-selection.\looseness=-1

